- [ ] **Multiplayer runs are blocked from the dev box's automation environment — WebRTC ICE never completes (2026-07-30).** Every MP attempt fails, down to `balancing:scan-multiplayer`'s cheapest 2-player config on an idle machine, which blocks the MP campaign re-run. **The harness's error message is misleading:** it reports `[attempt crashed] Target page, context or browser has been closed` and then `Error: browser appears dead: 3 consecutive fully-crashed batches` (`qualifyLoop.mjs:59`), but `DEBUG=pw:browser` shows the browser exiting with `exitCode=0` — the closed context is a *consequence* of the attempt being torn down. Real cause, from Chromium's own stderr: `sendto() has failed twice returning a transient error net::ERR_ADDRESS_UNREACHABLE` repeatedly plus `stun_port.cc: Binding request timed out` — outbound UDP is blocked, so `DEFAULT_STUN_URLS` (`stun:stun.l.google.com:19302`, `webrtcConnection.ts`) is unreachable and no peer connection is established. **Not a code regression:** GitHub Actions passes both `verify (multiplayer-transition)` and `verify (multiplayer-multiguest)` on the exact same commit. Ruled out: OOM (30Gi free, no oom-kill), `/dev/shm` (32G tmpfs at 1%), CPU contention (fails idle), disk (free space flat at ~5.08GB during a run — though the root fs is at **97%, 5.0GB free**, worth attention on its own). Worth investigating: `waitForIceGatheringComplete`'s doc comment claims a gathering timeout is tolerated and host candidates get used as-is, which is *not* what happens here — for two peers on one machine, loopback/host candidates alone ought to be enough. Two traps found alongside: `verify:multiplayer-connect` defaults `DEV_SERVER_URL` to `localhost:5173`, i.e. the developer's *own* dev server, so it is never a valid check of a branch (point `CODEENSTEIN_DEV_URL` elsewhere); and the laptop SSH lane can't run Playwright because `~/.bashrc`'s non-interactive-return guard sits above its nvm block, so `ssh host 'cmd'` gets system Node v18 despite nvm having v24 as default

- [ ] **Bot: the "oscillation" was mostly a bad detector, not a bot defect — diagnosis CORRECTED 2026-07-30. The recorded atan2 branch-cut root cause below is WRONG; read this first.** Reading one run's raw per-decision trace end to end (new `CODEENSTEIN_TELEMETRY_TRACE_DUMP=1`, plus `delta`/`navDist` on every trace record) contradicts the old diagnosis on every point:
  - **The sign does not flip every decision.** Measured across whole runs it flips on **14%** of decisions. The "flips decision to decision" claim came from a hand-picked five-line excerpt.
  - **The turn machinery is flawless.** A real captured turn: `+2.729 -> +2.274 -> +1.819 -> +1.364 -> +0.909 -> +0.454 -> 0.000` — exactly -0.455 rad per decision (50ms x 9.1 rad/s), landing dead on target. No overshoot, no branch-cut artifact. (The "overshoot" hypothesis floated in the attempt-2 revert is also wrong: all 44 `implausible rotation` warnings across six A/B runs are the *known* combat stall-strafe widening — computed burst 0.52ms, actual hold 50.0ms, 44/44 — which is rare and deliberately kept.)
  - **What the findings actually were:** (a) combat preempting navigation — the bot turns away from its waypoint to fight, gets strafed off course (`navDist` 0.24 -> 2.22), returns, re-engages (29-38% of ticks in 4 of 7 dumped runs); and (b) the bot's **ordinary gait** — `planRoute` emits one waypoint per tile, each corridor corner presents a 1.5-2.7 rad bearing change, and `MAX_WALK_WHILE_TURNING_RAD` (0.35) forbids holding `KeyW` above that, so the bot stops dead, turns in place for 3-6 decisions, sprints one tile, repeats. **28% of ticks inside flagged runs were frozen mid-turn.**
  - **Why both fixes "failed":** they were graded on a metric that was largely counting normal walking. Each slightly lengthened convergence, so more ticks accrued per turn and *more* windows qualified. That is the whole mystery of "two opposite interventions both made it worse".
  - **Fixed by** a route-progress exemption in `detectOscillation` (`6315051`): a run in which the bot reached >=2 nav targets is progress, not thrashing. Matched scan: **38 -> 4 findings**, other detectors unaffected (150 stall/healthDrainFrozen). Three of the four survivors cluster at one spot on `stage03_legacy_api.php` — (8.50,68.54), (8.18,68.44), (8.20,68.42) across separate attempts, `|delta|` flat at ~1.19, `navDist` stuck at ~1.2t, never arriving — a real localized wedge in the same y=68 corridor an earlier session hit, previously buried under 34 findings of ordinary walking.
  - **Open, and now the actual candidates:** (1) that level-3 wedge, which the detector finally isolates; (2) the stop-to-turn cost — the bot halts at nearly every tile boundary, so turning while moving is a real efficiency prize, though Stage 3's per-key hold durations tried something adjacent and was reverted for costing accuracy, so it needs the full A/B rather than enthusiasm.

- [x] *(superseded — kept only for the mechanism description, see the corrected entry above)* **oscillation on atan2's ±π branch cut; two fixes attempted, both A/B'd and REVERTED.** *Attempt 2* (`b540533`+`be311c4`, reverted `0f0bcde`): `plainNavTurnSign` pinned the turn direction while the heading sat on the cut. Full protocol, n=12/combo/side: oscillation **+10.9% / +23.4% / +2.6%** (Casual/Gamer/Pro), levelTime +7.1/+4.4/-4.9%, one guard breach (Gamer L1 stuck 0->1). Win condition inverted on 3 of 3. **Method lesson:** it was committed on an n=8 single-combo reading of **-37.4%**; the same combo at n=12 gave **+23.4%**. n=8 on one combo cannot carry a directional claim. *Attempt 1* (`9d49f4d` -> `0fb6f04`): gated `diagonalStrafeKey` on `strafeIsSafe`; findings 520 -> 570 (+9.6%) and inconsistent.

