**The registered win condition inverted on 3 of 3 combos.** Pro/hard did improve on route and time, but there is no win on the target metric to weigh that against, so the pre-registered rule applies: revert the stage, do not tune it forward. **Method lesson, the important one:** an earlier n=8 single-combo reading on Gamer/normal showed **-37.4%** and the change was committed on it; the same combo at n=12 gives **+23.4%**. n=8 on one combo cannot carry a directional claim — that is exactly what the 3-combo protocol is for, and labelling the result "directional, not gated" at commit time did not make shipping it correct. **What survives the revert** (kept deliberately, since it is what made the failure visible rather than being part of it): the tick-based anomaly tally in `anomalySummary`, `ticksPerKiloDecision` normalization, `diffAnomalies` in the A/B report, and `CODEENSTEIN_TELEMETRY_TUNING`. **What to try next:** both attempts so far removed or overrode a *symptom* of the flip (attempt 1 the strafe, attempt 2 the turn sign) and both made the detector fire more. That the two opposite interventions both backfire suggests the wobble is load-bearing convergence behaviour rather than waste, and the next attempt should probably target the overshoot that causes the sign to alternate at all (`turnBurstMs` sizing vs the decision window) rather than the sign itself. Original diagnosis follows —

